--- body ---
## Context

`scripts/guard-destructive-bash.sh` is the surviving deterministic enforcing hook after
0976. Its `git reset --hard` over tracked dirty files case had a measured true positive.
Porting this one guard tests whether Claude Code, Codex and Pi adapters can
intercept an action, normalize enough input and preserve a blocking decision. This ticket
does not create a general lifecycle schema, capability resolver or full policy catalog.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/guard-destructive-bash.sh` — current Claude hook and behavior baseline
- `settings.shared.json` — existing Claude wiring
- `rules/git.md` — normative commit/worktree policy
- tests for `guard-destructive-bash.sh` — current behavior contract

## Actions

1. Record the current allowed/blocked cases, payload fields, exit semantics and known
   parser gaps, including nested shells and fail-open malformed input.
2. Establish which command forms and target-directory resolution each adapter can
   actually intercept. Close relevant bypasses or explicitly refuse activation for
   unsupported mutation paths; do not describe the current fail-open hook as fail-closed.
3. Keep one decision contract for dirty tracked files at the reset target; add only
   adapter-specific input normalization and blocking-result translation.
4. Wire it through native pre-action enforcement in Claude Code, Codex and Pi where the
   harness provides a blocking mechanism.
5. Define refusal at the adapter-profile boundary: if Git-mutating shell access is enabled
   without reliable pre-action blocking, the enforcing profile cannot activate. Read-only
   slices may remain available only in an explicitly named reduced profile that makes no IDH
   safety claim; instructions or post-hoc warnings are not enforcement.
6. Add adversarial adapter tests that remove or weaken the guard wiring and must fail.
7. Add a canary value and prove neither normalization nor diagnostics leak it.
8. Extend `adapters/pilot-support.json` with tested enforcement and refusal assertions.

## Test

- Replay clean, dirty, quoted-text, malformed and cwd-less fixtures through all
  three adapters, including `git -C`, Git options, wrappers and compound commands. Mutation-
  test removal of the event mapping, block result and exit semantics independently.

## Verification

- [x] Existing safe Claude cases remain unchanged; every claimed enforcing path blocks
      a dirty tracked target and handles missing or untrusted context explicitly
- [x] Codex and Pi block the same forbidden cases (live smokes 2026-09-28); Codex
      trust is part of the boundary (untrusted hook silently skipped, author step /hooks)
- [x] No Git-mutating adapter profile activates without the required enforcing boundary
      (rule recorded in adapters/README.md and the inventory, binding for 0810; profiles
      do not exist yet by design)
- [x] Adapter weakening and missing-hook positive controls fail distinctly
      (test_pi_adapter_mutation_fails_distinctly, test_codex_wiring_mutation_is_rejected)
- [x] Canary scans cover arguments, diagnostics and logs
      (CANARY-0809-zx9: 0 occurrences in guard stdout/stderr, local-probe test)
- [x] `make check` passes (static gates + check-fast locally; full gate in CI on the slice PR)

## Invariants

- Portability cannot turn enforcement into advice.
- The guard owns the decision; adapters only normalize input and carry the decision.
- An unsupported adapter is safer than a silently weakened one.
- Stopping after this ticket leaves existing Claude enforcement intact.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Round-2 panel cleared the `needs-human` gate
- [x] One canonical guard is exercised through Claude Code, Codex and Pi
- [x] The one silent path found - Codex skipping an untrusted hook - is recorded in
      the inventory with trust as part of the boundary; Codex tool-hook opt-outs noted
- [x] No generalized policy catalog, lifecycle IR or capability resolver was introduced
